# AllowanceAgent — plan.md (Sprint 2)

## Feature Summary

Sprint 2 transforms AllowanceAgent from a single-parent local tool into a multi-user family system with revenue. Three workstreams: (1) The Learner role lets the child interact from their own Claude account — seeing their progress, completing OpenMAIC lessons (orchestrated by Claude), and self-reporting achievements with transparent source tagging. (2) HTTP transport enables multi-device connections so parent, child, and family members can all connect simultaneously. (3) x402 micropayment gating generates revenue on value-delivery tools while keeping onboarding and growth tools free. Five bug fixes from Sprint 1 evaluation close the remaining gaps.

## Architecture Decisions

### 1. Dual transport — stdio + HTTP
**Decision:** Support both via `TRANSPORT` env var. stdio for local Claude Desktop (backward compat), HTTP for multi-user + x402.
**Why:** Breaking existing stdio users is unacceptable. HTTP enables the child connection, family member connections, and x402 revenue — all of which require remote access.

### 2. Learner role is child-scoped, not globally scoped
**Decision:** Each Learner member is linked to a specific `childName`. Tool responses filter to that child only.
**Why:** Maya should not see her brother Carlos's progress. The scoping is enforced in the middleware, not in each tool handler — one check, applied everywhere.

### 3. Source field is logged, not enforced
**Decision:** Any caller can set any `source` value on `verify-achievement`. The system logs it transparently. The parent reviews source provenance during distribution.
**Why:** AllowanceAgent cannot verify that Claude actually called OpenMAIC — it can only trust Claude as the intermediary. Cryptographic verification is a V3 concern. For Sprint 2, the parent's review step is the trust gate.

### 4. x402 only in HTTP mode
**Decision:** stdio mode is free (no x402). x402 gating only applies when running in HTTP transport mode.
**Why:** Local Claude Desktop users (single parent) shouldn't need USDC to use their own tool. x402 is for the hosted/multi-user deployment model.

### 5. Passphrase moves to server-side env var
**Decision:** `OWS_PASSPHRASE` env var, set once at server startup. Tools read from env, never from user input.
**Why:** Fixes Bug #5. Passphrase no longer flows through tool args (visible in logs). In HTTP mode, remote callers never see or send the passphrase.

### 6. OpenMAIC integration is Claude orchestration, not code integration
**Decision:** No OpenMAIC dependency in AllowanceAgent. Claude orchestrates between the two services. AllowanceAgent just receives `source: "openMAIC"` in the metadata.
**Why:** Loose coupling. Either system can evolve independently. AllowanceAgent is a universal achievement sink.

### 7. Invite delivery is a Claude orchestration concern, not an AllowanceAgent concern
**Decision:** `invite-member` returns the invite code, the server URL, and a pre-formatted join message. Claude delivers it to the child using `message_compose` (SMS/iMessage) when the parent says "text it to her." AllowanceAgent does not send emails, push notifications, or manage delivery.
**Why:** Claude already knows how to send messages. Building email/SMS infrastructure inside AllowanceAgent is unnecessary complexity. The invite response provides all the content Claude needs to handle delivery. The `ALLOWANCE_AGENT_URL` env var tells the tool what server URL to include in the join instructions.

### 8. Three documented paths for child connection
**Decision:** Document three realistic ways the child connects their Claude to the AllowanceAgent server: (1) parent sets up child's device directly, (2) child adds MCP server URL to Claude Desktop config, (3) parent texts join instructions via Claude `message_compose`. No single path is required — families choose what works.
**Why:** A 9-year-old won't edit JSON config files. A 15-year-old will. A parent helping a 6-year-old will set up the device themselves. Supporting all three paths without requiring any specific one keeps the product accessible across ages.

